  <!-- SECTION 2: ALTITUDINAL ADAPTATION & TRAITS -->
  <section id="hilo-adaptation" aria-labelledby="hilo-adaptation-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="hilo-adaptation-title">Adaptation</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mgdb-panel">
<div class="hilo-adaptation-grid">
      <article class="hilo-adaptation-card">
        <h3>Phosphorus Efficiency &amp; Low-Temperature Growth</h3>
        <p>
          Mexican highland varieties \(such as Palomero Toluque&ntilde;o\) are cultivated at extreme altitudes where volcanic soils severely limit phosphorus availability. These varieties exhibit specialized mycorrhizal associations, increased purple anthocyanin pigmentation protecting photosynthetic machinery against intense ultraviolet radiation, and superior metabolic performance under low temperatures.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="hilo-adaptation-card">
        <h3>Macro-Hair Density &amp; Root Architecture</h3>
        <p>
          Highland maize produces dense macro-hairs \(trichomes\) covering leaf sheaths and stems, creating a micro-boundary layer that buffers against mountain frost and insect herbivory. Highland plants feature specialized root system development, and farmers historically practice hilling \(piling soil around the stalk base\) during vegetative growth to support anchorage and prevent lodging.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="hilo-adaptation-card">
        <h3>Elevational Genomic Divergence &amp; Flowering Time</h3>
        <p>
          Comparative mapping across the elevational gradient from 50 meters \(Zapalote Chico\) to over 2,500 meters \(Palomero Toluque&ntilde;o\) uncovers large chromosomal inversions and regulatory polymorphisms in photoperiod and flowering pathways \(e.g., <em>ZCN8</em>, <em>Vgt1</em>\), driving local adaptation to highland and lowland environments.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>

  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3: KEY PUBLICATIONS & ABSTRACT PREVIEWS -->
  <section id="hilo-publications" aria-labelledby="hilo-pub-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="hilo-pub-title">Publications</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mgdb-panel">
<div class="hilo-publications-grid">
      <!-- Publication 1: Perez-Limón et al. 2022 -->
      <article class="hilo-pub-card">
        <div class="hilo-pub-meta-top">
          <span class="hilo-pub-journal-badge">G3 \(Bethesda\) &bull; 2022</span>
          <span class="hilo-pub-doi">DOI&#58; 10.1093/g3journal/jkab447</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="hilo-pub-title">A B73 &times; Palomero Toluque&ntilde;o mapping population reveals local adaptation in Mexican highland maize</h3>
        <p class="hilo-pub-authors">Sergio Perez-Lim&oacute;n, Meng Li, Gina C. Cintora-Martinez, M. Rosario Aguilar-Rangel, M. Nicole Salazar-Vidal, Enrique Gonz&aacute;lez-Segovia, Kevin Bl&ouml;cher-Ju&aacute;rez, Alejandra Guerrero-Zavala, Bernardo Barrales-Gamez, Jonathan Carca&ntilde;o-Macias, et al.</p>
        <p class="hilo-pub-citation"><em>G3&#58; Genes, Genomes, Genetics</em> 2022 Feb; 12\(2\)&#58;jkab447.</p>
        
        <div class="hilo-pub-abstract-wrap">
          <h4 class="hilo-pub-abstract-heading">Abstract</h4>
          <p class="hilo-pub-abstract-text">Maize was domesticated in the warm lowlands of southwestern Mexico and subsequently adapted to the cold volcanic highlands of central Mexico and the Andes. Highland landraces display distinctive phenotypes including stem pigmentation, macro-hair development, modified root architecture, and cold tolerance. To characterize the genetic architecture of highland adaptation, we generated a recombinant inbred line population between B73 and the Mexican highland landrace Palomero Toluque&ntilde;o. Quantitative trait locus \(QTL\) mapping across multi-environment highland and lowland field trials identified key genomic regions governing anthocyanin pigmentation, flowering time, plant height, and tillering, providing insight into the genetic basis of altitudinal adaptation in traditional landraces.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="hilo-pub-actions">
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-primary" href="https://doi.org/10.1093/g3journal/jkab447" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Full Text on G3 &#8599;</a>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-secondary" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34994770/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed 34994770 &#8599;</a>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-quiet" href="/data_center/reference?author=Perez-Limon">MaizeGDB Record</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- Publication 2: Eagles & Lothrop 1994 -->
      <article class="hilo-pub-card">
        <div class="hilo-pub-meta-top">
          <span class="hilo-pub-journal-badge">Crop Science &bull; 1994</span>
          <span class="hilo-pub-doi">DOI&#58; 10.2135/cropsci1994.0011183X003400010002x</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="hilo-pub-title">Highland Maize from Central Mexico - Its Origin, Characteristics, and Use in Breeding Programs</h3>
        <p class="hilo-pub-authors">Howard A. Eagles, J. E. Lothrop</p>
        <p class="hilo-pub-citation"><em>Crop Science</em> 1994 Jan; 34\(1\)&#58;11&ndash;19.</p>
        
        <div class="hilo-pub-abstract-wrap">
          <h4 class="hilo-pub-abstract-heading">Abstract</h4>
          <p class="hilo-pub-abstract-text">Highland maize from the Central Mexican plateau \(elevations &gt;2000 m\) represents a unique germplasm pool characterized by rapid early growth under low temperatures, high tolerance to night frosts, and distinctive grain quality. This foundational review outlines the geographical distribution, evolutionary origins, morphological attributes, and potential of Central Mexican highland landraces for introducing cold tolerance and phosphorus efficiency into temperate hybrid breeding programs worldwide.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="hilo-pub-actions">
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-primary" href="https://doi.org/10.2135/cropsci1994.0011183X003400010002x" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Full Text on Crop Science &#8599;</a>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-quiet" href="/data_center/reference?author=Eagles">MaizeGDB Record</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>

  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4: DATA HOSTED AT MAIZEGDB -->
  <section id="hilo-data-hosted" aria-labelledby="hilo-hosted-title">
